    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ACE inhibitors like lisinopril work by blocking the enzyme angiotensin-converting enzyme, which is responsible for converting angiotensin I into the potent vasoconstrictor angiotensin II. By inhibiting this conversion, the drug prevents the stimulation of the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System, thereby reducing systemic blood pressure and sodium retention. This mechanism effectively relaxes blood vessels and lowers the workload on the heart, making it a cornerstone treatment for hypertension and heart failure.",
