    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into more detail about the specific steps in the RAAS cascade? Like, what happens to the aldosterone levels and how that affects the kidneys? I want to make sure I have the whole sequence down.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The RAAS cascade begins when the kidneys release renin in response to low blood pressure, which cleaves angiotensinogen into angiotensin I. ACE inhibitors block the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, a powerful hormone that constricts blood vessels and signals the adrenal glands to release aldosterone. Lower angiotensin II levels result in decreased aldosterone secretion, which reduces the kidneys' reabsorption of sodium and water, leading to increased urine output and further blood pressure reduction.",
      "source": "target",
