    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok thanks. one more thing—when the ACE is actually being inhibited, what's the physical way we describe that interaction at the molecular level? like how the drug and the enzyme actually interact to stop the process? is it just a simple blockage or something more complex?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ACE inhibitors typically act as competitive antagonists that bind reversibly to the active site of the angiotensin-converting enzyme, physically blocking the binding of the natural substrate angiotensin I. This competitive inhibition requires higher concentrations of the substrate to overcome the drug's effect, yet it ultimately prevents the catalytic conversion of the peptide chain. This molecular blockade effectively stalls the entire kinase-initiated cascade, placing the inhibitor in direct spatial competition with the physiological substrate for the enzyme's binding pocket.",
